    {
      "@id": "https://trustoverip.github.io/dtgwg-rahp-tf/id/RK-SC05",
      "@type": "Risk",
      "name": "Credential Schema Gap — Supported Consent and LPA Not Representable",
      "category": "Schemas",
      "lifecyclePhase": "Phase 3–4 / Ongoing",
      "description": "The current VRC, VMC, and IDVC schemas assume a single credential holder who is the same legal person as the consent-giver, the DID controller, and the community participant. The schemas have no field or structure for: (a) co-signed admission where the signatory is not a substituted decision-maker; (b) a delegation credential representing a registered LPA; (c) a capacity-status flag indicating that the holder currently operates under supported or substituted decision-making; or (d) a secondary notification recipient registered in the trust registry. These gaps mean the architecture literally cannot represent legal relationships that exist in the real world for an estimated 1.3 million people in England and Wales alone (LPA registrations as of 2025).",
      "harmType": [
        "Governance Failure",
        "Exclusion"
      ],
      "severity": "High",
      "likelihood": "High",
      "harm_description": "The credential architecture cannot serve a large class of legitimate participants without introducing impersonation-equivalent workarounds. Any attempt to accommodate these users without schema changes creates governance ambiguity, audit trail corruption (Margaret's actions appear as Arthur's), and potential violations of the Mental Capacity Act 2005 (which requires clear records of attorney decision-making). Standards bodies that adopt the DTG spec without addressing this gap will produce a credential ecosystem that is legally non-compliant in the UK, EU, and any jurisdiction with equivalent incapacity law.",
      "affectedMetrics": [
        "https://trustoverip.github.io/dtgwg-rahp-tf/id/M-09",
        "https://trustoverip.github.io/dtgwg-rahp-tf/id/M-31",
